# Designing a Controlled Continuous Deployment Pipeline

## Overview

After introducing Continuous Integration, the next objective was to automate deployments without sacrificing production stability.

Rather than creating a fully automatic deployment pipeline, I designed a controlled Continuous Deployment (CD) process. The goal was to reduce manual work while ensuring that production releases remained deliberate, predictable and easy to troubleshoot.

---

## The Challenge

The existing deployment process relied on manually connecting to the Linux server, updating the application and restarting services.

This approach worked for a small team but introduced several risks:

- Production deployments depended on manual steps.
- Deployment procedures were difficult to reproduce consistently.
- Infrastructure changes were becoming harder to track.
- Releasing a new version always carried the risk of human error.

Simply automating every deployment was not the right solution. The release process first needed clear control points.

---

## My Approach

Instead of deploying every successful commit automatically, I implemented a controlled deployment workflow.

The solution separates software validation from production deployment:

- Continuous Integration verifies that the application can be built successfully.
- Production deployment requires an explicit approval before any infrastructure changes occur.
- The deployment process rebuilds the application, replaces the running container and verifies that the service starts correctly.

This approach keeps deployments automated while ensuring that production changes remain intentional.

---

## Deployment Flow

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A[Developer Push] --> B[GitHub]
    B --> C[CI Validation]
    C --> D{Deployment Approval}
    D -->|Approved| E[Production Environment]
    E --> F[Rebuild Application]
    F --> G[Health Verification]
```

---

## Outcome

The deployment process became significantly more reliable.

The new workflow provides:

- Consistent deployment steps.
- Reduced manual intervention.
- Clear separation between testing and production.
- Better visibility into deployment history.
- Faster recovery when issues occur.
- Safer production releases through manual approval.

The infrastructure is now prepared for future improvements such as automated rollback, deployment notifications and database migration workflows.

